4
00:00:25,518 --> 00:00:47,171
ALEX: The little guys are winning because they think interest rates are about to fall. When rates fall, small-cap stocks, which carry more debt, suddenly become less toxic. It's the financial equivalent of your credit card company calling to say they're lowering your A P R out of the goodness of their heart. Spoiler: they're not.

5
00:00:47,171 --> 00:00:51,424
MAYA: Wait, so what's the actual gainer story today? Who's printing?

6
00:00:51,424 --> 00:01:13,076
ALEX: A M D up 4 point eight nine percent. Broadcom up 2 point four eight percent. Both semiconductor names. Tesla, Eli Lilly—all in the green. The chip cycle is still running hot. Meanwhile, Costco got absolutely demolished—down 2 point seven six percent—because apparently bulk warehouse vibes are out of favor when you're worried about recession.

7
00:01:13,076 --> 00:01:18,103
JAMIE: Costco down 2 point seven six percent? What else got hit?

8
00:01:18,103 --> 00:01:35,115
ALEX: UnitedHealth down 2 point four five percent, PepsiCo down 2 point two two percent, Chevron down 2 point zero nine percent. The V I X ticked up 1.21 to 16.79, which is still nothing—we're in the "market is bored but mildly concerned" zone.

9
00:01:35,115 --> 00:01:42,075
MAYA: So A M D chose violence today, Costco is having an identity crisis—did I get that right?

10
00:01:42,075 --> 00:01:58,701
ALEX: You got it exactly right. Brent crude just bounced back above one hundred dollars a barrel after Iran said they're gearing up for retaliation against U.S. strikes. This is the geopolitical risk premium in oil basically operating like a light switch.

11
00:01:58,701 --> 00:02:07,207
JAMIE: So oil was down earlier because we thought the Iran thing was cooling off, and now it's back above one hundred?

12
00:02:07,207 --> 00:02:18,033
ALEX: Correct. Traders are slamming buy and sell buttons like they're playing Whac-A-Mole. This is what happens when your entire commodity price discovery mechanism depends on geopolitical developments.

13
00:02:18,033 --> 00:02:21,513
MAYA: So how much is the premium actually worth?

14
00:02:21,513 --> 00:02:37,753
ALEX: That's the problem—nobody knows. The consensus guess is somewhere between seventy and eighty dollars without geopolitical chaos. So we're paying twenty to thirty dollars a barrel just for the privilege of living in a world where the Middle East exists.

15
00:02:37,753 --> 00:02:41,232
JAMIE: Twenty to thirty dollars a barrel is insane.

16
00:02:41,232 --> 00:02:52,832
MAYA: It's worse than that though, right? Because if you're a refiner or an airline, you can't hedge that. You can't buy insurance against "maybe Iran retaliates, maybe they don't."

17
00:02:52,832 --> 00:03:04,045
ALEX: Exactly. And here's the thing that nobody's talking about—the U.S. could resume military action if discussions collapse. That's not a peace signal. That's a threat with a smile.

18
00:03:04,045 --> 00:03:15,258
JAMIE: So what does this actually mean for energy stocks? Because we saw Chevron down 2.09 earlier, but if oil is back above one hundred, shouldn't they be rallying?

19
00:03:15,258 --> 00:03:25,311
MAYA: They should be, but they're not, which means the market thinks oil is going to tank again. Chevron's got commitment issues with this rally.

20
00:03:25,311 --> 00:03:33,817
ALEX: World's third-largest shipping line just reported earnings that got absolutely eviscerated. Container rates have fallen seventy percent from their pandemic peaks.
  ↳ source: https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/world-third-largest-shipping-line-142642236.html

21
00:03:33,817 --> 00:03:41,550
JAMIE: Seventy percent? That's not a correction, that's a capitulation. What does that actually signal about global trade right now?

22
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:51,989
ALEX: It signals that consensus estimates for growth are fantasy. When container rates crater like this, it means there's too much shipping capacity chasing too little cargo.

23
00:03:51,989 --> 00:03:57,402
MAYA: Wait, so shipping lines are the actual economic tell, not the stock market?
  ↳ source: https://www.investors.com/market-trend/stock-market-today/dow-jones-sp500-nasdaq-us-iran-deal-oil-prices-tumble/?src=A00220&yptr=yahoo

24
00:03:57,402 --> 00:04:07,455
ALEX: Shipping lines are always the tell. The market's pricing in rate cuts because inflation is "under control," but shipping data says demand is actually weak.

25
00:04:07,455 --> 00:04:14,415
MAYA: Iran's internet is coming back online after a months-long blackout. International access is back in limited capacity.

26
00:04:14,415 --> 00:04:22,148
JAMIE: They turned off the internet for months and then just... turned it back on? Like a bad WiFi router?

27
00:04:22,148 --> 00:04:24,855
MAYA: Exactly like a bad WiFi router.

28
00:04:24,855 --> 00:04:29,494
ALEX: So the takeaway is that totalitarian information control has serious limitations.

29
00:04:29,494 --> 00:04:46,120
MAYA: There's a shadowy hacking group that stole NSA hacking tools years ago and still nobody knows who they are or what they're doing with them. Like, we spent billions on cybersecurity architecture that's supposed to stop exactly this, and it did nothing.

30
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,440
JAMIE: Wait, they haven't been caught?

31
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:55,787
MAYA: Nobody even knows if they're active or dormant. The tools are floating around somewhere in the world.

32
00:04:55,787 --> 00:05:03,906
ALEX: So the lesson is: pay more to a vendor, sleep worse at night, and hope nobody tells you the truth.

33
